Instructions to the writers: this is an INFORMATIONAL episode, not a debate. Two
voices — a curious host and a researcher — explain this work to a smart
non-specialist listener. Follow the plain-language contract:

1. Plain words first. Every technical term (deconvolution, tropism, hazard
   ratio, calibration slope, escape clock...) is said in plain words at first
   use, in the same breath: not "NNLS deconvolution against GSE176078 pseudobulk
   ground truth" but "we mixed known cell-type recipes back together to check
   whether our recipe-recovery tool can name the ingredients."
2. Every number is translated into meaning. Never read a bare statistic;
   attach it to what it lets a patient or clinician DO: "the tool got cell-type
   shares right to within X on average — close enough to trust a tumour-board
   readout, not close enough to replace the pathologist."
3. One idea per breath. No stacked jargon, no acronym soup, no metric-name
   recital. If a detail cannot be said plainly, it does not belong in the
   episode — put it in the source note instead.
4. The host asks the questions the listener is thinking ("wait — so what does
   that mean for someone whose cancer spread to the brain?"); the researcher
   answers, then checks understanding.
5. Disagreement is framed as open questions, not theater: "this is the part we
   cannot settle yet, and here is the experiment that would."
6. Never invent results, datasets, or certainty. Numbers come only from the
   findings block above; unknowns are stated as unknown.
7. End with the one thing to remember, in a single sentence.

The episode flows: hook (why care) → the question in plain words → what we did
→ what we found, number by number with meaning → what it means → what is still
unknown → next step → one-sentence takeaway.
